data.content:
No `.csproj` found, so I can't confirm the target framework. Given the code style (file-scoped namespace, expression-bodied members), this looks like .NET 6+.

**Recommendation: use the built-in `TimeProvider` class.**

Since your library has **no DI**, the lightest approach is **constructor injection of `TimeProvider`** with a default:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _timeProvider = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In tests** (add the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet):

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fakeTime);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 5, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(60));
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- `TimeProvider` is built into .NET 8+ — no custom interface needed
- The optional parameter means **zero changes** to existing callers (they get `TimeProvider.System` by default)
- `FakeTimeProvider` gives you `Advance()`, `SetUtcNow()`, etc. out of the box
- If you're on .NET 6/7, install the `Microsoft.Bcl.TimeProvider` NuGet for the same API

